00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
remember pebble the smartwatch pioneer  that beat all the tech giants to the  punch between 2012 and 2015 pebble was  one of the most popular smart watcher  brands and they had a massive fan base  that supported them against challengers  like apple and samsung yet within just  15 months they went from being a  dominating player to being a large  player to being a laggard to shutting  down  their entire fall from glory didn't take  much longer than just a year so what  happened to pebble  taking a look back pebble wasn't some  sort of big tech company or some  billionaire's passion project pebble was  actually a startup founded by eric  mikikovsky through y combinator and  kickstarter at y combinator eric's  smartwatch idea caught the attention of  a couple of venture capitalists who  invested a total of 375 000  and though that was a solid amount it  was nowhere near enough to hire hardware  designers software engineers app  developers and everyone else involved in  creating a smart watch from scratch the  problem was that while most investors  were intrigued by the idea they weren't  willing to invest into the project  because pebble was largely a hardware  business and hardware businesses are  notorious for having razor thin profit  margins and large capital requirements  unable to convince more vcs eric decided  to take his idea to kickstarter and this  is where the brand would really take  shape tech enthusiasts on kickstarter  had no cure for the business side of  pebble they had fantasized about smart  watches for years and someone was  finally offering to make it a reality  not to mention pebbles offerings were  super cheap especially compared to  today's standards their retail price was  projected to be 150  but they were offering it for 115 to  kickstarter backers and 100 for the  first 200 backers in april of 2012 eric  launched a fundraiser with the target of  one hundred thousand dollars he would  end up raising the entire amount within  just two hours and over the next six  days pebble became the most funded  project in kickstarter's history raising  over 4.7 million dollars by the end of  the campaign pebble was able to raise  10.2 million dollars and with that they  were off to the races pebble began  working with a consulting firm called  the dragon innovation who helped them  identify suitable suppliers and  manufacturers the next several months  were not easy by any means as pebble ran  into a plethora of manufactured  difficulties and design limitations  but eric slowly addressed one issue  after another and he was eventually able  to launch the product in early 2013. the  watch was quite simple and nothing fancy  but what made the pebble watch stick out  was its insane utility pebble supported  email call and a text message  notifications and you could check stock  prices track workouts and monitor your  sleep it was basically just a standard  smart watch but in 2013 this was  phenomenal and the best part was that  since pebble opted for a minimal energy  black and white display they were able  to offer seven day battery life for 100  to 150 dollars backers were extremely  impressed and the pebble watch launched  to an uproar of positive reviews and  feedback eric didn't let this get to his  head though he was laser focused on  pushing out more applications and  improving the customer experience by  february of 2014 the pebble app store  boasted a total of 1 000 applications  but it wasn't all smooth sailing notable  players like samsung and motorola were  starting to roll out their own smart  watches so eric decided to fire back  with the second generation called the  pebble time eric had been closely  monitoring all the constructive feedback  he received on the original pebble watch  and he basically addressed every concern  with the pebble time in terms of  hardware the time offered a 64 color  display and it sported gorilla glass the  time also allowed users to swap the  stock straps with third-party straps  eric also introduced premium versions of  the time called the pebble time steel  and the pebble time round the pebble  time steel offered higher quality  finishes including steel and leather and  it offered a 10 day battery life  meanwhile the pebble time round offered  a more traditional looking watch  in february of 2015 eric once again went  to kickstarter to fund this launch and  the pebble would obliterate every record  their goal was to raise 500 000  they would reach this goal in just 17  minutes  and they would reach a million dollars  within 49 minutes  by the end of the fundraiser they raised  a total of 20 million dollars  in the meantime apple would reveal the  apple watch and the pebble fans laughed  at it apple expected people to pay 350  up to 17 dollars for a product that was  comparable to pebbles offerings not to  mention the apple watch only had a  battery life of 18 hours compared to  pebble's seven to ten day long batteries  eric was actually excited for the launch  of the apple watch he anticipated that  the apple watch would simply increase  the interest in smart watches and drive  up pebble sales and he was absolutely  right in the six months following the  release of the apple watch pebble sales  doubled they even turned down a 740  million acquisition offer from  watchmaker citizen  but just 15 months later the company  would shut down  so what in the world happened  well i think the first and biggest issue  bringing down pebble was their laser  focus on utility  from day one pebble was never trying to  produce a trendy or lifestyle product  they were trying to create the most  value-packed hardware they could  they could have easily thrown on an led  screen and cool animations but they  prioritized battery life over flashy  looks  at first this was the perfect approach  because the entire smartwatch community  was filled with a bunch of tech  enthusiasts whose primary focus was  utility  all they wanted was a long lasting  smartwatch that could display  notifications and useful information  that's it they weren't looking to flex  their watch and they couldn't care less  about what others thought about it but  as apple introduced the apple watch the  general public got involved and their  expectations were radically different  the average person buying an apple watch  doesn't actually use it for work-related  purposes  they're not tracking stocks throughout  the day and it's probably been months  since they sent their last email the top  two reasons that people buy napa watch  is for raw convenience and looks  they don't want to pull out their phone  to view text messages and they don't  want their phone checking around in  their pocket while they're working out  oh yeah and they like to see the little  activity circles fill up throughout the  day  they don't care about a seven-day  battery life and they don't want to look  at a bland display they want rich colors  and an intriguing ui and above all  whether they want to admit it or not  they want to flex  apple likes to brand themselves as a  lifestyle brand but we all know that  this just means that they're a flex  brand  now i can't really blame these guys  because i was basically describing  myself right there but this was really  bad news for pebble  the smartwatch space was rapidly growing  but all the consumers entering the space  weren't tech enthusiasts they were  general consumers who didn't really care  all that much about utility but then why  was pebble's sale skyrocketing after the  apple watch announcement  well i think that this was just an  impulse reaction to apple's crazy  pricing spending 350 to 400 on lapa  watch seemed ludicrous when there were  similar offerings for far less than half  the price  and that brings me into the second  reason behind pebble's downfall which is  a race to the bottom  after the launch of the apple watch the  number one thing that still attracted  the general public towards pebble  watches was price  other than battery life the apple watch  was slightly better than pebble's  offerings in every way  now was it twice as good not even close  but it was slightly better and it was  tied into the apple ecosystem  this resulted in people buying pebble  watches as a cheaper alternative that  offered more value per dollar  in other words the general public wasn't  buying pebble watches because they were  enthusiastic about the brand or products  they were simply settling with a pebble  watch and the worst part was that pebble  was simply encouraging people to upgrade  to an apple watch  as people wore pebble watches they  realized how convenient the smartwatch  was and the next time they bought a  smartwatch many of them decided to  splurge and spring for an apple watch so  peppa was almost serving as an  entry-level product that got people  hooked to make things even worse apple  started to be more competitive iphone  sales had hit a peak in the mid-2010s  and apple started taking diversification  much more seriously they offered much  lower entry prices and they kept older  generations around for much longer today  you can literally get a brand new apple  watch series 3 from their official  website for 200  and if you wait for black friday you can  easily pick up an apple watch for less  than 150  now of course this isn't the flagship  version but i'd be willing to bet that  most people would take an older  generation apple watch over a newer  generation pebble watch if they were  roughly the same price  so as apple brought out newer  generations it just became cheaper and  cheaper to get an apple watch  and given that price was the only thing  that attracted the general public to  pebble watches they left as soon as  apple met that need  and this was made extremely clear in the  holiday season of 2015. up until then  the release of the apple watch had been  boosting pebble sales but as soon as  apple watches were being offered for  substantial discounts throughout the  holiday season pebble sales plummeted in  the last quarter of 2015 pebble went  from doubling their sales in the last  six months to negative year-over-year  growth and let's keep in mind pebble  wasn't a profitable business  they were still in the growth phase and  they needed to achieve economies of  skill to produce sustainable  profitability if growth was slowing down  they were screwed eric tried to address  this decline in demand with the pebble 2  which debuted on kickstarter in may of  2016.  they still performed extremely well on  kickstarter but the excitement was  substantially lower than the last time  pebble was able to raise 12.7 million  dollars and while that's an insane  amount that's 40 less than last time and  for a startup that needed consistently  increasing demand this was the end eric  scrambled to find a company that could  acquire pebble but by this point  pebble's disappointing outlook was  pretty clear  the only company willing to buy pebble  was fitbit and their offer came in at  just 40 million dollars 700 million less  than just a year ago also fitbit wasn't  looking to save pebble or keep the brand  alive they were simply trying to  leverage pebble software and their team  so as soon as fitbit acquired pebble  they fired everyone except for the  software team and shut down their  company at the end of 2016 and that was  the end of pebble  honestly there wasn't really anything  that pebble could have done that much  better  sure they could have tended to the  desires of the general public but i  don't think that would have played out  much better either  pebble's monumental growth was thanks to  a lack of options along with great  execution by eric but the truth is no  level of execution could have  overpowered apple in the mid-2010s truly  a sad ending to a spectacular start  do you guys own an apple watch and if so  why do you choose apple over the  competition comment that down below also  drop a like if you think that pebble  will always be the og and of course  consider joining our discord community  to suggest future video ideas and  consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  you

